---
// src/pages/bones-seed-on-a-5070.astro
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import ByteMap from '../components/ByteMap.astro';
const title = '131 hours of motion on an RTX 5070';
const publishDate = 'November 13, 2026';

const stats = [
  { value: '12 GB', label: 'RTX 5070, a mid-range consumer GPU' },
  { value: '131 h', label: 'of motion, 23.6 M frames' },
  { value: '10.6 GB', label: 'in use while training, down from 40.3' },
  { value: '92 B', label: 'per frame of motion, down from 1.35 kB' },
];

const errors = [
  { what: 'Joint angles', err: '4.9e-5 rad' },
  { what: 'Body positions', err: '0.06 mm' },
  { what: 'Goal features', err: '5e-5 relative' },
  { what: 'Foot contact flags flipped', err: '0.004 %' },
];

const quantCode = `Q_MAX = 3.2  # rad: int16 fixed point over [-Q_MAX, Q_MAX], step 9.8e-5 rad

# when the library is built
q_i16 = np.round(np.clip(q, -Q_MAX, Q_MAX) * (32767 / Q_MAX)).astype(np.int16)

# when a frame is read
q = q_i16[f].astype(jnp.float32) * np.float32(Q_MAX / 32767)`;

const velCode = `def vel(self, f):
    c = self.clip_id[f]
    fp = jnp.minimum(f + 1, self.clip_end[c])     # stay inside the clip
    fm = jnp.maximum(f - 1, self.clip_start[c])
    dt = (jnp.maximum(fp - fm, 1) * CONTROL_DT)[..., None]
    v = (self.root_p[fp] - self.root_p[fm]) / dt
    ...`;
---
<Layout title={`${title} - Project`} active="projects" description="Training a humanoid motion tracker on the full 131-hour BONES-SEED library with one RTX 5070, a mid-range consumer GPU with 12 GB of memory.">
  <main class="page">
    <article class="wrap post">
      <header class="page-head">
        <div>
          <p class="kicker"><a href="/#projects">Projects</a> / bones-seed-on-a-5070</p>
          <h1 class="page-title">{title}</h1>
          <p class="page-caption">
            XMimic now trains on the whole feasible BONES-SEED motion set with one RTX 5070, a mid-range consumer
            GPU with 12 GB of memory. The same run used to need 40 GB. Nearly all of the difference was the motion
            library, which went from 32 GB to 2.2 GB.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="page-meta meta">
          <span>{publishDate}</span>
          <span>4 min read</span>
          <span>Python · JAX · MuJoCo · RL</span>
        </div>
      </header>

      <div class="zone lead-zone">
        <figure class="fig">
          <div class="fig-frame grow chart paper-light">
            <img width="1984" height="1264" src="/bones-seed-gpu-memory.png" alt="GPU memory before: 40.3 GB, of which the motion library is 31.9 GB. After: 10.6 GB, of which the motion library is 2.2 GB, which fits under the 12 GB of the RTX 5070" />
          </div>
          <figcaption>
            GPU memory with the motion library on the card, before and after. "Training" is the trainer's own state
            (4.7 GB) plus the compiled program's temporaries (3.7 GB), as measured with the compact library.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
        <div class="stats">
          {stats.map(s => (
            <div class="card stat">
              <span class="stat-value">{s.value}</span>
              <span class="kicker">{s.label}</span>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
      </div>

      <section class="post-sec">
        <header class="post-sec-head">
          <p class="kicker">01</p>
          <h2>A 12 GB card and 131 hours of motion</h2>
        </header>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            <a href="https://nico-bohlinger.github.io/papers/2026_xmimic_xsoma.pdf">XMimic</a> trains a humanoid
            motion tracker entirely on the GPU: the simulator, the replay buffer, the networks and the reference
            motions all live in device memory, and whole training iterations are compiled into one program. That is
            what makes it fast, and it means the motion library has to fit on the card too.
          </p>
          <p>
            With the 40 LAFAN1 clips this is a non-issue. <a href="https://huggingface.co/datasets/bones-studio/seed">BONES-SEED</a>
            is another scale. After dropping the clips that cannot be done on a flat, empty floor (more on that
            below), 64,697 of its 67,036 clips remain: 131 hours of motion, 23.6 million frames at the 50 Hz control
            rate.
          </p>
          <p>
            The GPU I have is an RTX 5070: a mid-range consumer card with 12 GB of memory. I wanted the whole set to
            train on it.
          </p>
          <p>
            The trainer holds 4.7 GB of its own state and the compiled program needs another 3.7 GB of temporaries,
            which leaves 3.6 GB of the card for motion data. The library stored 1.35 kB for each frame, about 32 GB
            in all. Together that is 40.3 GB, more than the 32 GB of an RTX 5090 at the top of the same lineup, so
            the run belonged on a 48 GB workstation or datacenter card.
          </p>
          <p>
            The rest of this post is how 32 GB of reference motion got into those 3.6 GB.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="post-sec">
        <header class="post-sec-head">
          <p class="kicker">02</p>
          <h2>Keep only what cannot be recomputed</h2>
        </header>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            Most of those 1.35 kB were conveniences. For every frame the loader precomputed the root's linear and
            angular velocity, the joint velocities, and the position, rotation and velocities of every tracked body.
            All of it is a function of three things: where the root is, how it is rotated, and the 29 joint angles.
          </p>
          <p>
            So the library now stores only those three, and the rest is computed at the moment a frame is indexed,
            with the same formulas the loader used:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Velocities</strong> are central differences of the neighbouring frames, one-sided at the two ends of a clip.</li>
            <li><strong>Body poses and velocities</strong> come from forward kinematics on the stored root and joints.</li>
          </ul>
          <pre class="code"><code>{velCode}</code></pre>
          <p>
            The derived fields still index like arrays (<code>motion.body_p[f]</code>, <code>motion.qd[f]</code>), so
            the reward and observation code did not change. Inside a compiled iteration the compiler merges the work
            that several fields share at the same frames.
          </p>
          <p>
            This alone takes a frame from 1.35 kB to about 180 bytes, and the library from 32 GB to 4.26 GB.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="post-sec">
        <header class="post-sec-head">
          <p class="kicker">03</p>
          <h2>Then squeeze what is left</h2>
        </header>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            4.26 GB is still about 0.7 GB more than the 3.6 GB the card has free. The remaining bytes went in four
            places:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Root rotation</strong> as a float32 quaternion instead of a 3×3 matrix: 16 bytes instead of 36.</li>
            <li><strong>Joint angles</strong> as int16 fixed point over ±3.2 rad: 58 bytes instead of 116, with a step of 9.8e-5 rad.</li>
            <li><strong>Clip index</strong> not stored at all. Every frame already knows its one-second reset bin, and the bin knows its clip.</li>
            <li><strong>Foot contacts</strong> as one byte per foot.</li>
          </ul>
          <figure class="fig">
            <div class="fig-frame pad paper-light">
              <ByteMap />
            </div>
            <figcaption>
              One frame of reference motion, before and after. The hatched part held velocities and the poses and
              velocities of the tracked bodies, all of which follow from the root and joint trajectories.
            </figcaption>
          </figure>
          <pre class="code"><code>{quantCode}</code></pre>
          <p>
            That is 92 bytes a frame and 2.18 GB for the 131 hours. Compared against the float32 library on the same
            frames, the largest differences are:
          </p>
          <div class="card table-wrap">
            <table>
              <thead>
                <tr><th>Quantity</th><th>Largest difference</th></tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                {errors.map(e => (
                  <tr><td>{e.what}</td><td>{e.err}</td></tr>
                ))}
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p>
            A sixteenth of a millimetre in body position is far below anything a tracking reward can feel.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="post-sec">
        <header class="post-sec-head">
          <p class="kicker">04</p>
          <h2>Training on the 5070</h2>
        </header>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            With everything precomputed the run needed 40.3 GB. Dropping the recomputable fields brought it to
            12.7 GB, within reach of the card but still over, and the quaternion and the int16 angles got it under
            the line at 10.6 GB: 2.2 GB of motion next to the trainer's 8.4 GB, with 1.4 GB of the card to spare.
          </p>
          <p>
            With that, XMimic trains on all 131 hours on the one RTX 5070, and I left it running overnight. What the
            policy learns from is the same as before: the reward and observation code read the same fields, to within
            the differences in the table above.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="post-sec">
        <header class="post-sec-head">
          <p class="kicker">05</p>
          <h2>What else broke at this size</h2>
        </header>
        <div class="prose">
          <h3>Clips that need furniture</h3>
          <p>
            A motion-capture set of this size is full of people sitting on chairs, climbing stairs and leaning on
            railings. The simulator has a flat, empty floor, so a robot asked to follow those clips can only fall.
            Clips whose description names a prop the body stands on, sits on or leans against are dropped; held or hit
            props stay.
          </p>
          <p>
            A second check works from the reference motion alone. It flags a clip when tracked bodies sink into the
            floor, when the body floats, or when a nearly still pose has the pelvis outside the support of the limbs
            on the ground, which means sitting on or leaning against something that is not there. Push-ups, planks,
            crawling and get-ups pass. 64,697 of 67,036 clips survive.
          </p>

          <h3>Sampling where to start</h3>
          <p>
            Each simulated robot restarts from a random one-second bin of the library, weighted by how badly the
            policy does there. The stock categorical sampler builds an array of random numbers with one entry per
            robot and per bin. With roughly half a million bins that array was its own memory problem, so the bins
            are now drawn by inverse-CDF sampling, which needs one number per robot.
          </p>

          <h3>Loading it at all</h3>
          <p>
            Parsing tens of thousands of CSV files and resampling them to 50 Hz takes a while, so files are parsed in
            parallel processes and the resampled clips are cached next to the data.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="post-sec">
        <header class="post-sec-head">
          <p class="kicker">06</p>
          <h2>Fine print</h2>
        </header>
        <div class="prose">
          <ul>
            <li>This post is about getting the training run onto the card. It makes no claim about how well the policy tracks all 131 hours.</li>
            <li>Deriving velocities and body poses on demand costs compute on every lookup. I have not measured how much it slows an iteration.</li>
            <li>Joint angles beyond ±3.2 rad would be clipped. The loader counts them and warns.</li>
            <li>The error figures compare the compact library with the float32 one, not with the original motion capture.</li>
            <li>"Fits" means 10.6 GB of the card's 12 GB, at the batch sizes of this run.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="post-sec">
        <header class="post-sec-head">
          <p class="kicker">07</p>
          <h2>Credits</h2>
        </header>
        <div class="prose credits">
          <p>
            Motion Data by <a href="https://bones.studio">Bones Studio</a>. The trainer is
            <a href="https://nico-bohlinger.github.io/papers/2026_xmimic_xsoma.pdf">XMimic</a> by Nico Bohlinger and Jan
            Peters, a JAX rebuild of <a href="https://github.com/fuyichen0725/blinkmimic">BlinkMimic</a> by Fuyi Chen;
            the method is <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28480">SuperMimic</a> (Liao et al.). The robot is the
            Unitree G1 and the physics is <a href="https://mujoco.org">MuJoCo</a>.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>
    </article>
  </main>
</Layout>

<style>
  .chart { display: flex; align-items: center; }
</style>
